::GEO_U06_L01_GP01::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U06_L01_GP01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.8<br><br>Question:<br>Which calculation is most useful for proving two sides of a coordinate quadrilateral are parallel? {
=A. Slope#Equal slopes support parallel sides in a coordinate proof.
~B. Midpoint#A midpoint supports bisection, not parallel sides.
~C. Perimeter#Perimeter gives total distance, not side direction.
~D. Area#Area does not prove that sides are parallel.
}

::GEO_U06_L01_GP02::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U06_L01_GP02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.8<br><br>Question:<br>A triangle has side lengths 5, 5, and 8 from distance calculations. Which classification is supported? {
~A. Scalene#A scalene triangle has no congruent sides, but two sides are equal here.
=B. Isosceles#Two equal side lengths support classifying the triangle as isosceles.
~C. Equilateral#An equilateral triangle needs all three sides congruent.
~D. Right#Side lengths 5, 5, and 8 do not satisfy the Pythagorean Theorem.
}

::GEO_U06_L01_GP03::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U06_L01_GP03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.8<br><br>Question:<br>Which evidence supports that diagonals bisect each other? {
~A. The sides have equal slopes#Equal slopes support parallel sides, not diagonal bisection.
~B. The side lengths are all different#Different side lengths do not prove diagonal bisection.
=C. The diagonals have the same midpoint#If both diagonals share a midpoint, each diagonal bisects the other.
~D. The figure appears symmetric#Appearance is not enough for coordinate proof.
}

::GEO_U06_L01_GP04::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U06_L01_GP04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.8<br><br>Question:<br>A coordinate proof shows adjacent side slopes are 2 and -<sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>2</sub>. What relationship is supported? {
~A. The sides are parallel#Parallel sides have equal slopes, not negative reciprocal slopes.
~B. The sides are congruent#Slope does not measure length.
~C. The sides are bisected#Bisection requires midpoint evidence.
=D. The sides are perpendicular#Negative reciprocal slopes support perpendicular sides.
}

::GEO_U06_L01_GP05::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U06_L01_GP05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.8<br><br>Question:<br>Why is 'it looks like a rectangle' not enough in a coordinate proof? {
=A. A rectangle classification needs coordinate evidence such as perpendicular slopes and side relationships#Coordinate proof must support conclusions with calculations.
~B. The graph cannot show any shape#A graph can show a shape, but conclusions still need evidence.
~C. Rectangles cannot be proved using coordinates#Coordinate geometry can prove rectangle properties.
~D. Only area can classify a rectangle#Area alone does not prove rectangle properties.
}
